# Hostile audit — global signed penetration matching

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_global_signed_penetration_matching_2026-09-15.md`  
**Disposition:** **PASS at proof-only current low-`t` full-core Type-II scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The claimed gain is a physical matching, so the failure modes are sharper than for a
mere edge count. This audit checks the three nontrivial steps: nearby route occurrences
must have nearly equal hull normals, a common penetration endpoint must have
multiplicity at most two, and the opposite endpoint of a penetration must lie outside
the straight-vertex set.

---

## 1. Nearby route occurrences force nearby hull normals

For an occurrence `x` on route `W_i`, the previously hostile-audited signed
penetration proof gives

\[
|x-M_i|\le s_i+d_i/2,
\]

where `M_i` is the midpoint of the corresponding loaded-hull side, `s_i` its side
length, and `d_i` the route detour. The uniform loaded-side collapse gives
`max_i s_i=o(D)`.

For occurrences `x,y` with `|x-y|=O(1)`, triangle inequality gives

\[
|M_i-M_j|
\le s_i+s_j+O(1)+(d_i+d_j)/2.
\]

Full-core near-disk convergence places the side midpoints at radius `D/2+o(D)` and
aligns their radial directions with the side normals up to `o(1)`. If `theta` is the
normal-angle separation,

\[
D\sin(\theta/2)
\le(d_i+d_j)/2+o(D).
\]

Since `d_i+d_j<=d_out`,

\[
\sin(\theta/2)\le\delta/2+o(1),
\qquad
\delta=d_{out}/D.
\]

At the current endpoint `delta<=0.002883975+o(1)`, so

\[
\theta<0.0028841+o(1).
\]

The argument uses only a fixed `O(1)` bound on `|x-y|`; it therefore applies both to
points sharing a unit edge and to two penetration tails sharing one unit-distance
endpoint.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 2. A common endpoint receives at most two penetrations

For a `15`-degree straight penetration `x_aw`, put `e_a=w-x_a`. Signed inwardness gives

\[
e_a\cdot n_a>1/\sqrt2,
\]

so `e_a` lies within `pi/4` of its hull normal `n_a`.

If several penetrations share `w`, their outer endpoints are pairwise at distance at
most two. Section 1 makes all associated normals lie in an angular window of width
`o(1)`. Therefore all `e_a` lie in one interval of width

\[
\pi/2+o(1)<2\pi/3.
\]

At `w`, the contact rays toward the outer endpoints are `-e_a`. Unit separation gives
pairwise angular separation at least `pi/3`; equality would make the two outer
neighbors unit-separated from each other and form a forbidden unit equilateral
triangle with `w`. Thus all pairwise separations are strictly greater than `pi/3`.

Three directions in an interval shorter than `2pi/3` are impossible: after ordering
them, the first-to-second and second-to-third gaps would already sum to more than
`2pi/3`.

Hence one endpoint receives at most two penetrations.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 3. The opposite endpoint cannot itself be `15`-degree straight

Let `xw` be an extra signed penetration at straight vertex `x`. Suppose `w` also
supports a `15`-degree straight occurrence. Since `|x-w|=1`, Section 1 makes the two
associated hull normals asymptotically equal.

If `xw` is extra at `w`, signed inwardness points the same physical edge in opposite
directions against two almost equal normals, immediately impossible.

If `xw` is a route edge at `w`, its absolute normal component there is strictly less
than

\[
\sin(\pi/12)=0.258819\ldots.
\]

Changing the normal from `n_w` to `n_x` costs at most

\[
|n_w-n_x|\le0.002884+o(1).
\]

Thus the edge's inward component relative to `n_x` is below `0.262`, contradicting the
required value `>1/sqrt2=0.7071...`.

So the straight-tail set and the opposite-endpoint set are physically disjoint.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 4. The matching conclusion is graph-theoretically exact

Every `15`-degree straight vertex has contact degree three or four and exactly two
route incidences, hence one or two extra penetration incidences. Thus its degree in the
all-extra penetration graph is at most two.

Section 2 gives opposite-endpoint degree at most two. Section 3 makes the two endpoint
classes disjoint. The penetration graph is therefore bipartite with maximum degree
two, hence a disjoint union of paths and even cycles. Its edges split into two
matchings, so

\[
\nu\ge |E|/2.
\]

Using the proved all-extra edge floor

\[
E/D\ge1.1543116921171727469\ldots-o(1)
\]

gives

\[
\nu/D\ge0.5771558460585863734\ldots-o(1).
\]

The same argument survives restriction to any edge subfamily. Applying it to the
non-singleton-rhombic-train penetration family gives

\[
M_{R,15}/D\ge0.1190397115637251974\ldots-o(1),
\]

and to the simple-outer rhombic-train family gives

\[
M_{R,simp,15}/D\ge0.0960714416082082916\ldots-o(1).
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 5. Scope

The matching does not imply distinct train support: many vertex-disjoint penetration
edges can belong to one long non-singleton train. Nor does it imply that moving the
matched endpoint pairs independently preserves the other active contact constraints.

What it removes is physical endpoint reuse. Any later simultaneous-deformation or
train-occupancy argument may now take a linear subfamily in which the penetration
contacts themselves share no source vertices.

**Final verdict:** **PASS. C-II remains open.**
